Lab 11: Integrating Cloud-Native Services with Camel K
Objectives
Deploy and run Camel routes in Kubernetes/OpenShift using Camel K
Integrate Camel routes with OpenShift Kubernetes resources (ConfigMaps and Secrets)
Utilize OpenShift's scaling and health-check features for integration applications
Prerequisites
OpenShift 4.x cluster with cluster-admin access
oc CLI installed and configured
Camel K operator installed in the cluster
kamel CLI installed locally
Basic knowledge of Kubernetes concepts (Pods, Deployments, Services)
Basic understanding of Apache Camel concepts
Setup Instructions
Verify Camel K operator installation:
oc get pods -n openshift-operators | grep camel
Create a new project for this lab:
oc new-project camel-k-lab
Task 1: Deploy Camel Routes as Serverless Functions
Subtask 1.1: Create a Basic Camel K Integration
Create a new file named hello.groovy:
// camel-k: language=groovy

from('timer:hello?period=3000')
    .setBody().constant('Hello from Camel K!')
    .to('log:info')
Deploy the integration:
kamel run hello.groovy --dev
Expected Outcome: You should see log output every 3 seconds showing the message "Hello from Camel K!"

Subtask 1.2: Make the Integration Serverless
Modify the integration to scale to zero when idle:
kamel run hello.groovy --annotation camel.apache.org/force-scaling=true
Verify the Knative service:
oc get ksvc
Troubleshooting Tip: If the integration doesn't scale down, check the Knative serving operator is installed.

Task 2: Integrate with OpenShift Resources
Subtask 2.1: Use ConfigMaps for Configuration
Create a ConfigMap:
oc create configmap my-config --from-literal=message="Hello from ConfigMap!"
Create config-route.groovy:
// camel-k: language=groovy
// camel-k: config=configmap:my-config

from('timer:config?period=5000')
    .setBody().simple('${configmap:my-config/message}')
    .to('log:info')
Deploy the integration:
kamel run config-route.groovy
Expected Outcome: The logs should show "Hello from ConfigMap!" every 5 seconds.

Subtask 2.2: Use Secrets for Sensitive Data
Create a secret:
oc create secret generic my-secret --from-literal=password=supersecret
Create secret-route.groovy:
// camel-k: language=groovy
// camel-k: config=secret:my-secret

from('timer:secret?period=10000')
    .setBody().simple('Password is ${secret:my-secret/password}')
    .to('log:info')
Deploy the integration:
kamel run secret-route.groovy
Security Note: The secret value will be visible in logs - this is for demonstration only.

Task 3: Utilize OpenShift Scaling and Health Checks
Subtask 3.1: Configure Horizontal Pod Autoscaling
Create scaling-route.groovy:
// camel-k: language=groovy

from('timer:scaling?period=1000')
    .setBody().constant('Testing auto-scaling')
    .to('log:info')
Deploy with scaling parameters:
kamel run scaling-route.groovy \
    --trait hpa.enabled=true \
    --trait hpa.max-replicas=5 \
    --trait hpa.metrics=memory
Verify HPA:
oc get hpa
Subtask 3.2: Add Health Checks
Modify scaling-route.groovy to add health checks:
// camel-k: language=groovy
// camel-k: trait=health.enabled=true

from('timer:health?period=1000')
    .routeId('health-route')
    .setBody().constant('Health check test')
    .to('log:info')
Redeploy:
kamel run scaling-route.groovy
Check health endpoints:
oc get pod
oc exec <pod-name> -- curl -s localhost:8080/q/health
Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Successfully deployed Camel K integrations as serverless functions
Integrated Camel routes with OpenShift ConfigMaps and Secrets
Configured auto-scaling and health checks for your integrations
These skills enable you to build robust, scalable integration solutions in cloud-native environments using Camel K.

Cleanup
To clean up all lab resources:

kamel delete --all
oc delete configmap my-config
oc delete secret my-secret
oc delete project camel-k-lab
